$# – Used to count the total number of command-line arguments passed to the script.

-ne – Used to check whether two numbers are not equal.

echo – Used to display the usage message on the terminal.

$0 – Represents the name of the shell script.

$1 – Represents the first command-line argument (filename).

$2 – Represents the second command-line argument (starting line number).

$3 – Represents the third command-line argument (ending line number).

sed -n – Used to print only the specified lines of a file.

"${start},${end}p" – Specifies the starting and ending line numbers to be displayed.

"$file" – Represents the filename stored in the variable.

if and then – Used to execute commands based on a condition.

fi – Used to mark the end of the if statement.
